<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Additional Model Details</h2>

<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">VAE Training Architecture</h5>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A6.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ VAE Training Architecture ‣ Appendix F Additional Model Details ‣ LaDiR: Latent Diffusion Enhances LLMs for Text Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> is a more in-depth diagram of the training of the VAE. The encoder LLM first maps the input sequence of original text embeddings and learnable embeddings into hidden states. These hidden states are then projected through two linear layers to produce the mean and variance of the latent distribution, from which we sample the thought tokens via the reparameterization trick. The sampled latent tokens <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tilde{z}_{1},\tilde{z}_{2},\ldots,\tilde{z}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tilde{z}_{1},\tilde{z}_{2},\ldots,\tilde{z}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> are passed to the decoder LLM, which reconstructs the original text tokens under a teacher-forcing setup. This design enables the model to compress high-dimensional text into a smaller set of semantically meaningful latent variables, while still maintaining faithful reconstruction of the original reasoning process.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04573v1/figs/vae_fig.png" id="A6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:494/336;" width="494" height="336" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Detailed architecture of the variational autoencoder for latent reasoning. The encoder is a finetuned LLM that takes both original text embeddings <math id="A6.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(w_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(w_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and learnable embeddings <math id="A6.F7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{z}(z_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{z}(z_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, producing mean and variance vectors through linear projections of the last hidden state <math id="A6.F7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. Latent thought tokens <math id="A6.F7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are then sampled from <math id="A6.F7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mu,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. The decoder is a frozen LLM that reconstructs the original CoT text under teacher forcing, conditioned on both the sampled thought tokens and the original text embeddings.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Use of Large Language Models (LLMs)</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this project, Large Language Models were utilized as coding tools and as grammar checkers to support the writing of the paper. They did <span id="A7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">not</span> play a significant role in research ideation or writing to the extent of being listed as a contributor. LLMs were used strictly as a general purpose tool.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
